{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.measured_target_reachable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.measured_target_reachable","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L176-L181","doc":"The measured body-order target of 2026-08-29, normalised to `a₂ = 1`:\n`a₃ = −0.519787`, `a₄ = +4.45038`. It is reachable, hence not a test. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_five_free","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_five_free","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L169-L174","doc":"`c₀` remains free after `a³` and `a⁴` are fixed, so `a⁵` is reachable too. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.depth_three_kernel_reaches","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.depth_three_kernel_reaches","kind":"theorem","line":159,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L159-L167","doc":"**The three-term target carries no information.** For a nonzero two-body\nterm, every pair of 3- and 4-body values is realised by some depth-3 kernel, via\n`c₂ = t₃/a₂` and `c₁ = (t₄ − t₃²/a₂)/a₂`. Matching the measured ratio therefore\n*solves for* the kernel rather than testing it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_six","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_six","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L146-L151","doc":"`a⁶` is the first value determined by earlier terms once the whole kernel is\npinned — the first genuinely falsifiable body order at depth 3. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_five","kind":"theorem","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L140-L144","doc":"`a⁵` involves `c₀`, which `a³` and `a⁴` leave unconstrained. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_four","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L133-L138","doc":"At depth 3 with a vanishing one-body term, `a⁴ = c₁ · a² + c₂ · a³`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.a_three","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L126-L131","doc":"At depth 3 with vanishing 0- and 1-body terms, `a³` is `c₂ · a²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.no_equivariant_prolongation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.no_equivariant_prolongation","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L112-L118","doc":"The `S₅`-equivariance dichotomy: an equivariant prolongation operator on\n`H¹(Bring) ≅ W₍₄,₁₎^{⊕2}` has `d ≤ 2` by Schur, which is inconsistent with the\nvanishing one-body term. See the module docstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.three_le_depth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.three_le_depth","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L100-L110","doc":"**Depth ≥ 3 is forced.** A vanishing zero- and one-body term together with a\nnonzero two-body term put a *lower* bound on the recurrence depth. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.eq_zero_of_initial_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.eq_zero_of_initial_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L83-L98","doc":"A depth-`d` recurrence whose first `d` terms all vanish is identically zero.\nNo hypothesis on the kernel: the vanishing propagates through any `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.IsDepthRecurrence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.IsDepthRecurrence","kind":"def","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BodyOrderRecurrenceDepth.lean#L72-L77","doc":"A depth-`d` linear recurrence with `n`-independent kernel `c`:\n`a (n + d) = ∑_{j < d} c j * a (n + j)` for every `n`. This is the shape\n`prop:prolonged-periods-fixed-depth` derives from Cayley–Hamilton on the\nprolongation operator. ","signatureChunk":0}]}